  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 224 AM MDT Mon Aug 10 2026\n\nThe Four Corners high will remain in place, along with a zonal flow \npattern over the Cowboy State today. Similar conditions from Sunday \nwill return across the area with temperatures remaining above normal \nand critical fire weather conditions in place. Westerly winds, with \ngusts of 25 to 35 mph, will increase after 18Z across much of the \nCWA. The notable exception will be most areas of the Bighorn Basin, \nwhere winds will struggle to gust up to 20 mph. Virga showers/dry \nthunderstorms will also develop over the southern half of the \nforecast area early in the afternoon, with the Wind River Mountains, \nthe northern end of the Salt River/Wyoming Ranges and the western \nhalf of Sweetwater County having the best chances for a shower or \nstorm. Wind gusts up to 55 mph will be possible due to large \ndewpoint depressions that will be in place once again this \nafternoon. Most of the convection will end across the area by \nsunset. Red Flag Warnings have been reissued for areas east of the \nDivide and southern portions of the CWA from 18Z to 03Z Tuesday \n(12PM to 9PM) due to aforementioned critical RH values, gusty winds \nand dry thunderstorms. Skies will remain hazy from smoke will \ncontinue today, especially over northern portions. \n\nAnother day of hot and dry conditions return Tuesday. Highs in the \nmiddle 90s will occur again for most areas east of the Divide and \nupper 80s/lower 90s west of the Divide. Wind gusts of 25 to 35 mph \nwill occur again, but are not expected to be as widespread. Red Flag \nWarnings will likely only be needed for a few zones as a result. \nNeedless to say, the hazy/smoky skies will continue across the area \nas well. \n\nThe pattern will begin to shift Wednesday, as the Four Corners high \nweakens and erodes and the flow aloft over the Cowboy State turns \nmore southwesterly irt an approaching trough. Precipitable water \nvalues will increase through the day as a result, with values \napproaching 1\". This won't be as strong as the surge of monsoonal \nmoisture that occurred a few weeks ago in July, but the threat for \nlocally heavy rain/localized flash flooding will certainly be in \nplace. Convection looks to be rather isolated so far, but \ntemperatures will be slightly cooler from increased cloud cover and \na weak cold front. This trend continues into Thursday, with \ntemperatures dropping into the 70s to near 80 degrees. Thursday also \nlooks to be the best chance for widespread precipitation, especially \nacross western portions of the state, as the trough moves over ID. \nThe cooler conditions continue Friday, as the trough exits over the \nNorthern Plains. Convection will be more widely scattered as weak \nshortwaves move over the area through the continued southwest flow \naloft. The threat for locally heavy rain will also continue as the \nmonsoonal moisture stays in place. This trend will continue through \nthe weekend, as shortwaves continue to rotate over the region from \nthe southwest.",
